I(71,[71],'idea of the earlier run',[42,50],'1084;1232','Brauer–Severi ... trisection','An étale degree-three divisor D on a smooth genus-zero X/S gives O(D)⊗ω of fibre degree1, whose rank-two direct image identifies X with a projective bundle.','This removes the Brauer obstruction but does not trivialize the rank-two bundle or order D.','agrees','The degree computation is 3−2=1 and relative H⁰ has rank2; no preferred coordinate follows.')
I(72,[72],'idea of the earlier run',[40,42,48],'1043;1084;1201–1207','bisection; √i','For i invertible, the fixed bisection has quadratic class i but the ordering torsor’s sign quotient has class −3i because r=F/(t(t−1)) is alternating and r²=−27i.','The two covers differ by the cyclotomic class −3.','agrees','Explicit substitutions by 1/t and 1−t give the alternating sign and explain the i=1 arithmetic twist.')
I(74,[74],'unpursued implication',[20],'453–479','Lemme d’Artin-Rees','For noetherian A, ideal m, finite E and submodule F, m^pE∩F=m^(p−k)(m^kE∩F) for p≥k; intrinsic and induced topologies coincide.','The retained proof starts the Rees-module argument but stops before finite generation of the intersection module.','agrees','The statement has the needed hypotheses; completion links to absent cote44 are separate leads.')
I(75,[75],'unpursued implication',[22],'552–558','toute série ... terme général tend vers0','A countably filtered abelian module is complete iff every series with terms tending to0 converges, using the ultrametric inequality.','Completeness does not include separatedness in this typescript.','agrees','The explicit maximum inequality proves the Cauchy criterion; it is false for arbitrary metric vector spaces.')
I(76,[76],'reading question',[22,24],'560–567;630','on ne suppose pas A séparé','An arbitrary quotient of a complete countably filtered module is complete in the possibly nonseparated sense; a closed submodule is complete.','For separated completion of a quotient, quotient by the closure is understood.','agrees','The coefficientwise proof explicitly permits nonunique sums, resolving the apparent contradiction.')
